Chapter: NetDrainBudget
  cites Foreword chapter Console
  cites Net chapter NetIO
  cites Net chapter NetworkStack

 Two waits, two caps, and they must sit on OPPOSITE sides of the give-up
 ladder. A loop waiting for a peer to appear has to outlast give-up or it
 ends by fuel and returns what an ordinary timeout returns; the send drain
 has to end BEFORE give-up, because it runs once per 1400-byte chunk and a
 stage that ends the run has cost the sitting whatever it was measuring.

 The ladder is derived here rather than written as 288, so moving
 `net-rto-max-tries` or `net-rto-max-ticks` moves this arm's answer with it.
 A literal would keep printing 288 while the thing it describes changed.

 The last row is the one that fails if somebody collapses the two caps back
 into one: `below` and `above` cannot both read `y` for the same number.

Section: Entry

  ladder : Integer
  ladder = net-rto-max-ticks * (net-rto-max-tries + 1)

  opening : [Console] Nothing = act
    print-line-uni ("ladder    : " & show ladder)
    print-line-uni ("drain     : " & show net-io-drain-ticks)
    print-line-uni ("wait      : " & show net-io-max-ticks)
    print-line-uni ("drain below: " & (if net-io-drain-ticks < ladder then "y" else "n"))
    print-line-uni ("wait above : " & (if net-io-max-ticks > ladder then "y" else "n"))
    print-line-uni ("drain cycles: " & show (net-io-drain-ticks / net-rto-max-ticks))
  end
